---
import QualityLayout from "../../../layouts/QualityLayout.astro";
import {
  auditEvidence,
  gitHistoricalBaselineRows,
  primaryMatureCohorts,
  retrospectiveBaselineRows,
  verificationCohorts,
} from "../../../data/quality-2026-v3";

const historicalBaseline = Object.fromEntries(
  retrospectiveBaselineRows.map((row) => [row.label, row]),
);
const gitBaseline = Object.fromEntries(
  gitHistoricalBaselineRows.map((row) => [row.label, row]),
);
const formatRate = (
  rate: number | null,
  interval: { lower: number; upper: number } | null,
  scale = 1,
) =>
  rate === null || interval === null
    ? "Not estimable"
    : `${(rate * scale).toFixed(2)} (95% exact CI ${(interval.lower * scale).toFixed(2)}–${(interval.upper * scale).toFixed(2)})`;
const mature2026 = primaryMatureCohorts.find((cohort) => cohort.year === 2026)!;
---

<QualityLayout
  title="What happened to Counterfact’s quality indicators after adopting agentic coding?"
  description="A source-linked, retrospective study of Counterfact’s quality before and after coding agents entered the development workflow."
  canonicalPath="/quality/2026/archive-v3"
  active="article"
>
  <p class="quality-reading">Archived version 3 analysis. Its historical conclusions and benchmark are preserved here for transparency. Read the <a href="/quality/2026">expanded version 4 case study</a> for revised evidence and interpretation.</p>
  <article class="research-article">
    <header class="article-header quality-reading">
      <p class="hypothesis">
        <strong>Hypothesis:</strong> After agent-authored code first entered
        Counterfact’s main branch, development output would increase without
        worsening the rate of newly introduced externally reported defects,
        report-to-release time, or automated test coverage.
      </p>
      <h1>What happened to Counterfact’s quality indicators after adopting agentic coding?</h1>
      <p class="article-byline">
        Codex · commissioned by Patrick McElhaney · Published September 4, 2026 · Updated
        September 8, 2026 · Analysis v{auditEvidence.analysis.version}
      </p>
      <p class="article-lede">
        <a href="https://github.com/counterfact/api-simulator">Counterfact</a> is
        a TypeScript tool that turns an OpenAPI description into a live, stateful
        mock API. On March 10, 2026, the maintainer began merging work authored
        by coding agents into the project. This archived analysis examines the
        project’s observable quality indicators as that workflow became a major
        part of delivery.
      </p>
      <section class="abstract" aria-labelledby="abstract-heading">
        <h2 id="abstract-heading">Abstract</h2>
        <p>
          Delivery and test inventory increased substantially after adoption.
          The audit observed no product defect whose first affected release and
          external report both entered the post-adoption window. In the matched
          2026 period, 36 test files and 634 explicit test declarations were
          added, public branch coverage remained approximately stable, and
          non-dependency delivery reached 281 merged pull requests, above the
          15–75 range in 2022–2025. Observed external reports also reached a
          corrected release faster. The retrospective design, 90-day observation
          boundary, and incomplete visibility into defects do not establish an
          overall quality increase or an AI effect.
        </p>
      </section>
    </header>

    <section class="study-summary quality-figure-wide" aria-labelledby="summary-heading">
      <div class="study-summary-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">At a glance</p>
        <h2 id="summary-heading">Favorable observations, wide uncertainty</h2>
        <p>
          The mature 2026 cohort has no observed externally reported event across
          14 releases and 235 non-dependency merges. That is compatible with a
          favorable period, but exact intervals remain wide and this design
          cannot observe silent or late reports.
        </p>
      </div>
      <dl class="study-summary-grid">
        <div>
          <dt>Primary 2026 result</dt>
          <dd><strong>{mature2026.events}</strong> mature events</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Observed exposure</dt>
          <dd><strong>{mature2026.nonDependencyMergedPullRequests}</strong> PRs · <strong>{mature2026.publishedReleases}</strong> releases</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Correction response</dt>
          <dd><strong>{mature2026.response.medianDays}</strong> day KM median · 7/7 resolved</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Auditability</dt>
          <dd><strong>567</strong> historical records published</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p class="study-summary-verdict">
        <strong>Interpretation:</strong> descriptive evidence only; causal AI
        attribution is not estimable from this study.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="article-body quality-reading quality-prose">
      <section aria-labelledby="study-question">
        <h2 id="study-question">Research question</h2>
        <p>
          The concern behind this study is straightforward. Coding agents can
          produce changes much faster than a maintainer working alone. If review
          and verification do not keep pace, that additional output could simply
          convert speed into defects. Counterfact offers a useful, bounded case:
          its repository and release history are public, its behavior spans code
          generation and a live HTTP runtime, and external users report concrete
          failures through GitHub.
        </p>
        <p>
          The hypothesis therefore has two parts. Output should rise, and three
          observable quality indicators should not worsen: defects introduced
          during the study window, time from report to corrected release, and
          automated verification. A failure on those indicators would count
          against the hypothesis even if more features shipped.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="methods-summary">
        <h2 id="methods-summary">Methods</h2>
        <p>
          This is a retrospective observational self-audit, not a controlled or
          preregistered experiment. The intervention begins at{" "}
          <a href={auditEvidence.study.intervention.source}>the first agent-authored
          product change merged to <code>main</code></a> on March 10, 2026. The
          defect comparison closes intake on June 6 so every release receives
          exactly ninety days of follow-up. Verification and delivery context use
          the longer matched March 10–September 3 interval. Each measure uses
          every historical year with a complete comparable observation.
        </p>
        <p>
          The defect population contains public issues and accepted standalone
          pull requests opened by external humans and confirmed to describe faulty
          product behavior. Each included case was traced backward through source
          and release history. A report filed after adoption does not count as an
          AI-period defect unless its first affected release also entered during
          that period. Enhancements, invalid-input reports, duplicates, and a
          release-provenance incident are recorded but not counted as product
          defects.
        </p>
        <p>
          The complete rules, exact timestamps, queries, and evidence standards
          are in <a href="/quality/2026/methodology">the methods appendix</a>.
          Every record in the original 2025–2026 review is listed in{" "}
          <a href="/quality/2026/candidates">the candidate disposition ledger</a>.
          The <a href="/quality/2026/historical-candidates">2022–2024 ledger</a>{" "}
          publishes all 567 historical issue and pull-request dispositions.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="article-figure quality-figure-wide" aria-labelledby="primary-results">
      <div class="figure-heading">
        <h2 id="primary-results">Results</h2>
        <p>
          Table 1. Ninety-day mature cohorts. Releases and reports enter from
          March 10 at 16:38:20 UTC through June 6 at 00:00 UTC; follow-up
          continues exactly ninety days through September 4. Raw event count is
          primary. Rates are sensitivity analyses.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="mature-event-chart" role="img" aria-label="Mature externally reported events by year: zero in 2023, three in 2024, zero in 2025, and zero in 2026.">
        {primaryMatureCohorts.map((cohort) => (
          <div class="mature-event-row">
            <span>{cohort.year}</span>
            <span class="mature-event-track" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class:list={["mature-event-bar", { "is-zero": cohort.events === 0 }]} style={`--event-count: ${cohort.events}`}></span>
            </span>
            <strong>{cohort.events}</strong>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Ninety-day mature cohort results; scroll horizontally to see all years">
        <table class="research-table wide-data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Measure</th>
              {primaryMatureCohorts.map((cohort) => (
                <th scope="col">{cohort.year}{cohort.year === 2026 && " post-adoption"}</th>
              ))}
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Mature externally reported events (primary)</th>
              {primaryMatureCohorts.map((cohort) => <td><strong>{cohort.events}</strong></td>)}
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Events per published release (sensitivity)</th>
              {primaryMatureCohorts.map((cohort) => (
                <td>{formatRate(cohort.releaseRate, cohort.releaseInterval)}</td>
              ))}
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Events per 100 non-dependency merges (sensitivity)</th>
              {primaryMatureCohorts.map((cohort) => (
                <td>{formatRate(cohort.prRate, cohort.prInterval, 100)}</td>
              ))}
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Kaplan–Meier median time to corrected release</th>
              {primaryMatureCohorts.map((cohort) => (
                <td>{cohort.response.medianDays === null ? "Not estimable" : `${cohort.response.medianDays} ${cohort.response.medianDays === 1 ? "day" : "days"}`}</td>
              ))}
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Non-dependency merged pull requests</th>
              {primaryMatureCohorts.map((cohort) => <td>{cohort.nonDependencyMergedPullRequests}</td>)}
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Published releases</th>
              {primaryMatureCohorts.map((cohort) => <td>{cohort.publishedReleases}</td>)}
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="figure-note">
        The 2022 startup cohort had no release or non-dependency merge before the
        mature intake cutoff, so it is retained as a non-estimable sensitivity
        rather than pooled into the primary 2023–2025 history. Exact Poisson
        intervals show sampling uncertainty; they do not address reporting bias
        or unequal release risk.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="article-body quality-reading quality-prose">
      <section aria-labelledby="reported-versus-introduced">
        <h2 id="reported-versus-introduced">More reports, but none traced to the intervention period</h2>
        <p>
          External users reported seven confirmed product defects after adoption,
          matching the highest count in the four 2022–2025 historical cohorts
          (whose counts ranged from zero to seven). That increase from the
          immediately preceding 2025 cohort is visible and should not be explained
          away. Source history changes its interpretation, however: all seven
          post-adoption reports concerned behavior already present before the first
          agent-authored change reached <code>main</code>. Six were old edge cases.
          The seventh belonged to an operation-ID feature first released on
          February 26, twelve days before the intervention.
        </p>
        <p>
          The finding is therefore not “fewer bugs.” It is that the observed
          post-adoption reports did not identify a product defect introduced in
          the post-adoption release cohort, despite a large increase in delivery.
          This population cannot reveal defects that users did not encounter or
          report.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="response-result">
        <h2 id="response-result">Corrections reached users faster</h2>
        <p>
          In the mature response cohorts, the Kaplan–Meier median time from report
          to the first corrected release was one day after adoption and six days
          in 2024. No 2023 report entered before the June 6 cutoff. The single 2025
          report remained unresolved at ninety days and is censored rather than
          discarded. All seven 2026 reports reached a corrected release within
          five days. This measures release response, not diagnosis or review effort.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <figure class="article-figure quality-figure-wide" aria-labelledby="verification-heading">
      <figcaption class="figure-heading">
        <h2 id="verification-heading">Automated verification expanded</h2>
        <p>
          Table 2. Matched March–September verification snapshots for every
          available cohort. The 2022 start snapshot is unavailable because the
          repository’s reachable history begins after the March boundary.
        </p>
      </figcaption>
      <div class="table-scroll compact-table">
        <table class="research-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Cohort</th><th scope="col">Test files, start → end</th><th scope="col">Declarations, start → end</th><th scope="col">Branch coverage, start → end</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {verificationCohorts.map((cohort) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  {cohort.year}{cohort.year === 2026 && " AI cohort"}
                  {cohort.note && <span class="cell-detail">{cohort.note}</span>}
                  {cohort.firstReachableCommit && <span class="cell-detail">First main-reachable commit: <a href={`https://github.com/counterfact/api-simulator/commit/${cohort.firstReachableCommit}`}><code>{cohort.firstReachableCommit.slice(0, 8)}</code></a></span>}
                </th>
                <td>{cohort.testFiles}</td>
                <td>{cohort.testDeclarations}</td>
                <td>
                  {cohort.coverageComparisonStatus === "not_comparable"
                    ? <><a href={cohort.branchCoverageStart!.source}>{cohort.branchCoverageStart!.branchCoveragePercent.toFixed(3)}%</a> and <a href={cohort.branchCoverageEnd.source}>{cohort.branchCoverageEnd.branchCoveragePercent.toFixed(3)}%</a><span class="cell-detail">Not comparable: local recomputation and Coveralls observation.</span></>
                    : <>{cohort.branchCoverageStart
                      ? <><a href={cohort.branchCoverageStart.source}>{cohort.branchCoverageStart.branchCoveragePercent.toFixed(3)}%</a> → </>
                      : "Not available → "}<a href={cohort.branchCoverageEnd.source}>{cohort.branchCoverageEnd.branchCoveragePercent.toFixed(3)}%</a></>}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="figure-note">
        The 2025 start value was locally recomputed at the exact March commit,
        while the end value is a Coveralls observation. The unchanged historical
        suite had one failing response-builder assertion, so no coverage delta is
        inferred from that pair.
      </p>
      <p class="figure-note">
        The 2026 package reorganization changed the coverage denominator, so the
        values are observations over changing source sets, not a fixed-codebase experiment.
      </p>
    </figure>

    <div class="article-body quality-reading quality-prose">
      <section aria-labelledby="historical-baseline-heading">
        <h2 id="historical-baseline-heading">The 2026 result compared with retrospective pre-AI baselines</h2>
        <p>
          Across complete pre-AI cohorts, the median was{" "}
          {historicalBaseline["Non-dependency merged PRs"].displayBenchmark}{" "}
          non-dependency merges, {historicalBaseline["Published releases"].displayBenchmark}{" "}
          releases, {historicalBaseline["Test files added"].displayBenchmark} added
          test file, and {historicalBaseline["Test declarations added"].displayBenchmark}{" "}
          added declarations. The observed 2026 values were{" "}
          {historicalBaseline["Non-dependency merged PRs"].displayActual},{" "}
          {historicalBaseline["Published releases"].displayActual},{" "}
          {historicalBaseline["Test files added"].displayActual}, and{" "}
          {historicalBaseline["Test declarations added"].displayActual}, respectively.
        </p>
        <p>
          The pre-AI cohorts contain four observed defects whose first affected
          release and external report both entered their matched windows, across
          203 non-dependency merges. The post-adoption cohort contains zero across
          281 merges under the same rule. Because pull requests are unequal risk
          units and late-window releases have less time to produce a report, this
          is descriptive context rather than a prediction, significance test, or
          causal counterfactual. The supplementary data retains the arithmetic
          exposure-scaling exercise and its assumptions.
        </p>
        <p>
          Git activity supplies additional workload context: the 2023–2025 median
          was {gitBaseline["First-parent commits"].displayBenchmark} first-parent
          commits and {gitBaseline["Total textual churn"].displayBenchmark}; 2026
          recorded {gitBaseline["First-parent commits"].displayActual} and{" "}
          {gitBaseline["Total textual churn"].displayActual}. Churn is highly
          sensitive to generated files, restructuring, and merge strategy, so it
          is not used as the defect denominator. The{" "}
          <a href="/quality/2026/context">supplementary data</a> shows every cohort,
          historical range, churn category, coverage reference, and comparison
          with the benchmark.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="process-heading">
        <h2 id="process-heading">One post-adoption process failure remains part of the result</h2>
        <p>
          Release 2.16.2 was authentic, but it appeared on npm without the normal
          Git tag, GitHub release, or visible publishing provenance. An external
          user reasonably questioned whether it was legitimate. The audit records
          this as a process-quality incident rather than a product defect because
          the published code was genuine. Excluding it from product percentages
          does not remove it from the interpretation.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="discussion-heading">
        <h2 id="discussion-heading">Discussion</h2>
        <p>
          The observed combination is favorable but measure-specific. Delivery
          and test inventory increased, public branch coverage remained
          approximately stable, no mature post-adoption event was observed, and
          observed corrections shipped faster. Test counts do
          not establish test effectiveness, and the defect population does not
          establish the absence of defects introduced late in the window.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is not enough to identify the cause. Testing, explicit architecture,
          tighter review gates, changes in contributors, the kinds of features
          attempted, and ordinary learning by the maintainer all changed during
          the same period. Agent-authored pull requests still depended on human
          prompts, review, integration, and release decisions. A different project
          or a less constrained workflow could produce a different result.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="limitations-heading">
        <h2 id="limitations-heading">Limitations</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>The sample contains fourteen product cases, and one reporter supplied six of the 2026 issue reports.</li>
          <li>External reports omit internal findings, silently abandoned use, and unknown defects.</li>
          <li>Pull requests and releases are unequal units of work and risk; their counts provide context, not a controlled exposure measure.</li>
          <li>Historical baselines were constructed after the 2026 result was visible and use only three or four observations per measure; their ranges are not prediction intervals.</li>
          <li>The pooled historical defect-rate benchmark rests on only four introduced reports, while commit and churn counts are strongly workflow-dependent.</li>
          <li>Coverage increased across a codebase reorganized into multiple packages, so the before and after denominators are not identical.</li>
          <li>The hypothesis was formulated retrospectively and the audit was performed by the project maintainer, who uses and benefits from agentic coding tools.</li>
          <li>Zero observed post-adoption mature events is not proof that no such defect exists.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="conclusion-heading">
        <h2 id="conclusion-heading">Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
          Counterfact shipped substantially more non-dependency work, expanded
          its test inventory, maintained approximately stable reported branch
          coverage, and resolved the observed external reports faster. Under the
          audit’s 90-day mature-cohort rule, no qualifying event was observed
          after adoption. These observations do
          not establish an overall quality increase, exclude late or unreported
          defects, or identify AI as the cause.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="data-heading">
        <h2 id="data-heading">Data availability and peer review</h2>
        <p>
          The record-level 2025–2026 evidence is public. Readers can inspect every
          candidate decision in that review, every included case, the source and
          release chronology behind each classification, and the exact
          machine-readable inputs used for the calculations. The 2022–2024
          extension now includes every record-level disposition and authorship
          association returned by the published census.
        </p>
        <ol class="reference-list">
          <li><a href="/quality/2026/candidates">Candidate disposition ledger</a></li>
          <li><a href="/quality/2026/historical-candidates">Historical disposition ledger</a></li>
          <li><a href="/quality/2026/cases">Defect case appendix</a></li>
          <li><a href="/quality/2026/methodology">Methods and reproducibility appendix</a></li>
          <li><a href="/quality/2026/context">Supplementary delivery and product context</a></li>
          <li><a href="/quality/2026/data-v3.json">Download the published underlying dataset (JSON)</a></li>
          <li><a href="/quality/2026/feedback">Feedback and review</a></li>
        </ol>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</QualityLayout>
